**Pact (consumer side):**
```java
@ExtendWith(PactConsumerTestExt.class)
@PactTestFor(providerName = "user-service", port = "9000")
class UserClientPactTest {

    @Pact(consumer = "order-service")
    RequestResponsePact getUserPact(PactDslWithProvider builder) {
        return builder
            .given("user 42 exists")
            .uponReceiving("get user by id")
            .path("/users/42")
            .method("GET")
            .willRespondWith()
            .status(200)
            .body(new PactDslJsonBody()
                .integerType("id", 42)
                .stringType("name", "Alice")
                .stringMatcher("email", ".+@.+\\..+", "alice@example.com"))
            .toPact();
    }

    @Test
    void shouldFetchUser(MockServer mock) {
        UserClient client = new UserClient(mock.getUrl());
        User user = client.getUser(42L);
        assertThat(user.name()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }
}
```

This generates `target/pacts/order-service-user-service.json` — the contract.

**Publish to broker:**
```bash
mvn pact:publish -Dpact.broker.url=https://broker.example.com
```

**Pact (provider side):**
```java
@Provider("user-service")
@PactBroker(host = "broker.example.com")
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class UserServicePactVerifyTest {

    @LocalServerPort int port;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp(PactVerificationContext ctx) {
        ctx.setTarget(new HttpTestTarget("localhost", port));
    }

    @TestTemplate
    @ExtendWith(PactVerificationInvocationContextProvider.class)
    void verifyPact(PactVerificationContext ctx) {
        ctx.verifyInteraction();
    }

    @State("user 42 exists")
    void userExists() {
        userRepo.save(new User(42L, "Alice", "alice@example.com"));
    }
}
```

The `@State` methods are how the provider arranges its data for each precondition the contract declares.

**Spring Cloud Contract (alternative for Java-only stacks):**

Groovy DSL contract (in provider's repo):
```groovy
// src/test/resources/contracts/get_user.groovy
Contract.make {
    description 'should return user 42'
    request {
        method 'GET'
        url '/users/42'
    }
    response {
        status 200
        body([id: 42, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com'])
        headers { contentType('application/json') }
    }
}
```

The build:
- Generates JUnit tests on the provider that verify the real endpoint matches
- Publishes a **stub jar** (mock server) to Maven repo
- Consumer pulls the stub jar and uses it in tests:
```java
@AutoConfigureStubRunner(
    ids = "com.example:user-service:+:stubs:6565",
    stubsMode = StubRunnerProperties.StubsMode.LOCAL)
class OrderServiceContractTest { ... }
```

**Pact vs Spring Cloud Contract:**
- **Pact**: cross-language (JVM, Node, .NET, Python, Go, Ruby); Pact Broker is the standard; consumer-driven model; great for polyglot orgs
- **Spring Cloud Contract**: Java-first; provider-side DSL; great for Java-only ecosystem; integrates with stub-runner for consumers

**Message contracts (Kafka / RabbitMQ):**

Both Pact and Spring Cloud Contract support **async / message** contracts:
- Producer publishes events; consumer subscribes
- Contract: 'when event X happens, payload looks like Y'
- Test: producer test asserts event matches contract; consumer test runs against generated stub

```java
@Pact(consumer = "analytics-service", provider = "order-service")
MessagePact orderCreatedEvent(MessagePactBuilder builder) {
    return builder
        .given("an order is created")
        .expectsToReceive("order created event")
        .withContent(new PactDslJsonBody()
            .stringType("orderId")
            .decimalType("amount")
            .timestamp("createdAt"))
        .toPact();
}
```

**Bidirectional contracts (Pact 2023+):**

New approach where the provider publishes its **OpenAPI spec** and the consumer publishes its **Pact contract**; broker compares them automatically. Useful when one side can't or won't write Pact tests.
